Here is an example failure:

```
java.lang.AssertionError: Attempt 11 failed.
            Error(s):
        1. Wrong value at index 2 (expected 2, was 3)
        2. Wrong value at index 3 (expected 4, was 5)
        3. Wrong value at index 6 (expected 8, was 9)
        Notifications: [OnNextNotification[0], OnNextNotification[1], OnNextNotification[3], OnNextNotification[5], OnNextNotification[6], OnNextNotification[7], OnNextNotification[9]]
```

I understand that a subscription that is actively producing signals may continue to propagate those signals to the downstream when disposed of concurrently (i.e. disposal is "eventually consistent"). But I would expect that any signals emitted during or after the race would preserve their original ordering. In other words, once `delay()` has caught up to its newly-disposed state and does _not_ emit its `N`th `onNext` signal, it seems wrong for it to go on to emit its `N+1`th signal.

